**Knowledge check**

0/5 answered

**1. Why do we use \`input: history\` instead of sending only \`req.body\`?**

To reduce API costs

To provide conversation context to the AI

To authenticate the customer

**2. What does \`store: false\` do?**

Deletes our JavaScript history array

Prevents the Responses API from storing the response as a retrievable response object

Stops the AI from generating a response

**3. Why do we use \`historyQueue\`?**

To serialize history operations

To store messages permanently

To connect Express to OpenAI

**4. What happens when \`history.length = 0\` executes?**

Only the last message is deleted

The array is completely cleared

The array becomes undefined

**5. What is the problem with the current global history array?**

It cannot store strings

All customers share the same conversation

It automatically deletes messages

Submit answers

My suggestion for your next step: understand how to replace this global array with **session-based conversation history using Redis**. That will take you from a basic AI chatbot to a much more realistic backend architecture.